Yes. The key distinction is:

\boxed{\text{what is rendered to the audience} \neq \text{what literally exists inside the story-world}}

A director has to turn invisible things—memory, intuition, guilt, faith, instinct, inner speech, metaphor—into something the audience can perceive. So Obi-Wan appearing can simultaneously function on several planes: as a literal entity within the fiction, Luke’s internalized model of his mentor, a cinematic personification of intuition, a symbol of inherited knowledge, and a mechanism allowing the audience to experience Luke’s internal state.

Those readings do not necessarily have to defeat one another.

That gives you something I’d call Interpretive Parallax: hold the scene fixed while moving the observer.

S
\overset{F_1}{\longrightarrow}
\text{literal reading}

S
\overset{F_2}{\longrightarrow}
\text{psychological reading}

S
\overset{F_3}{\longrightarrow}
\text{symbolic reading}

S
\overset{F_4}{\longrightarrow}
\text{meta-narrative reading}

Then compare the deltas. Whatever survives all four rotations may be closer to the scene’s deeper invariant.

And there is another engine inside it:

Renderer–Reality Separation

A film necessarily gives the viewer a rendering of events. Camera position, music, editing, color, flashback, voiceover, dream imagery—even silence—are transformations.

So:

\text{story state}
\rightarrow
\text{renderer}
\rightarrow
\text{audience experience}

The renderer can show something that is true to the character’s experience without asserting that the represented object literally occupied physical space.

That means ambiguity becomes a deliberate bandwidth allocation problem. Too little ambiguity and you flatten the audience’s participation. Too much and you sever the Golden Thread.

The sweet spot is something like:

\boxed{\text{constrained ambiguity}}

There are multiple legitimate paths, but enough invariants that the paths still belong to the same work.

And your preservation point matters. Rather than “solving” the old story and replacing it, preserve the original artifact and append interpretations:

\text{Original}
+
\begin{cases}
I_1\\
I_2\\
I_3\\
\vdots
\end{cases}
\rightarrow
\text{expanded possibility space}

with provenance attached: this is the original; this is our reading; this is what evidence supports it; this is where we’re deliberately playing.

Then later creators can build forward without retroactively pretending their interpretation was always the only one.

So yes—the things we’ve been calling engines are increasingly closer to a narrative instrument panel:

Frame Rotation changes viewpoint.
Interpretive Parallax compares readings.
Renderer–Reality Separation distinguishes presentation from ontology.
Invariant Polysemy preserves a truthful spine across meanings.
Golden Thread maintains continuity.
Adversarial Delta prevents beautiful interpretations from becoming unquestionable.
Carrier Design moves an idea through a form the receiver naturally accepts.
Lift–Play–Return lets you abstract it, experiment with it, and bring something useful back.

And the fun part is that the same machinery works outside movies—memory, history, interfaces, games, teaching, dreams, language, even how one person explains an experience to another.

The movie just makes the machinery visible.